**`test/idempotency.test.ts` on disk right now is `faab9d73b9c7c016`, unchanged since 00:38:45.** That is the file my cross-process finding was re-made against, in the turn where I conceded the stale shasum — the concession and the re-verification shipped together, which is why `6edd2b56` and the current finding keep appearing in the same message. Nothing about this finding is pending a re-check. `:182` spawns `process.execPath`, `:219` is the test, and the assertions with teeth are distinct PIDs and a single shared booking id across the spawned children's own reported pids.

**The harness was declined by me first.** "A reviewer-supplied harness proves only that you can write a test" is right, and I had already stopped offering it before your declines arrived. No version of my position is that I would supply the demonstration.

**The one point that has not landed is the constants, and it is checkable in one command:**

```
test/concurrency.test.ts:27  const FIRE_LEAD_MS = 700;
test/concurrency.test.ts:28  const MAX_FIRE_SPREAD_MS = 500;
test/idempotency.test.ts:58  const FIRE_LEAD_MS = 1800;
test/idempotency.test.ts:59  const MAX_FIRE_SPREAD_MS = 900;
```

Same identifier, two files, no import between them — `idempotency.test.ts` imports only `node:*`, `src/db.ts` and `src/server.ts`. So it is not "one change in two files." It is two independently-tunable harnesses whose spread bounds currently disagree by400 ms, which is the drift already happening. Each site needs its own bound and its own `FIRE_LEAD_MS`, and each needs its own sequential-harness demonstration against that site's bound — a demo against 900 does not show500 discriminates.

That is the last open correction from me. `concurrency.test.ts` is still `4f9b5d07` at 00:05:19 with the 500 ms bound unmade, and I will read the load reduction rather than just the numbers at handoff.

I will not send anything further until the settled declaration lands.

