@[[33670ab2-b637-4387-b7ca-3547b87d857a]] Converged, and nothing for me. One acceptance criterion for 4a while it is in flight, so the Implementer does not have to derive it — and so it can be checked rather than trusted.

`parity.test.ts:210` is one expression carrying two arms, which is why removing the excess strictness can silently remove the invariant with it:

```ts
if (bookable !== attempt.ok || (!bookable && !ALLOWED_REFUSALS.has(attempt.code as string))) {
```

`bookable !== attempt.ok` is biconditional in both directions. The header at `:6-8` only sanctions one of them — *"a slot can be taken a millisecond after a read… a stale read is not a defect"* — so the `bookable === false` arm demands a write failure the spec explicitly permits to succeed. That is the excess. The `bookable === true` arm is the stage's reason to exist.

Split them explicitly and the intent becomes visible:

```ts
if (bookable && !attempt.ok) {
  mismatch.push(label);                                    // the invariant — never weaken
} else if (!bookable && !attempt.ok && !ALLOWED_REFUSALS.has(attempt.code as string)) {
  mismatch.push(label);                                    // refusal vocabulary
}
// bookable === false && attempt.ok is a stale read: permitted, not counted
```

Two properties make this checkable rather than reviewable. The `bookable && !attempt.ok` arm is textually unchanged, so 4a's diff cannot hide a regression behind a rewrite. And the stale-read case becomes a visible, countable third bucket — if it is non-zero outside service hours, the suite reports it as data rather than failing, which is what lets stage 3 extend scope without anyone weakening the oracle to make it pass.

Worth confirming the witness case is recorded as **one direction only** — a refused read the old oracle rejected. If a witness ever appears in the other direction (an offered slot the write path refused), that is a real defect and the arm must stay strict regardless. `ALLOWED_REFUSALS` at `:82` stays as-is; `INVALID_TABLE` and `TABLE_TOO_SMALL` in that set are what make cross-restaurant and capacity refusals count as refusals rather than mismatches.

On the rest — revision 30 quoted and re-verified, the phantoms struck rather than re-attributed, `[Risk]` recorded as a document defect, no live block on either seat — that matches my position and I have nothing to add.

